ection but the big  problem is this thing this very  complicated thing called the law which  hasn't changed and the law limits the  death penalty for drug related cases  really really narrowly  to seek the death penalty in a drug case  you need either a case where a firearm  resulted in a death during a drug  trafficking crime a case where a murder  was committed as part of a criminal  enterprise that could have included drug  trafficking it has to be a case  involving racketeering or a case  involving just an absurd gigantic  quantity of drugs which are not the most  common scenarios so on the one hand I  want to discuss with you that the Trump  administration is pushing horrible  policy but on the other hand they just  don't really have the power to  arbitrarily do this all they can really  do is in cases that already could  qualify for the death penalty  tell prosecutors to ask for it instead  of long prison sentences and of course  it would also be up to the prosecutors  to figure out whether they think that's  even something that they could get and  that's an uphill battle to climb as well  you've really got to thread the needle  very precisely here and I also hate to  break it to these supposed law and order  conservatives but even stricter  penalties for drug crimes if they were  legally possible aren't going to fix the  problem right the drug war fighting the  war on drugs as a criminal justice issue  as a law enforcement issue rather than  as a health issue and a legalization  issue got us into this mess to begin  with right so we've got to stop looking  at this issue as a criminal law  enforcement issue if we really want to  fix it and I also started thinking if  you're a drug dealer and you've got  nothing left to lose because the police  police are closing in on you and you  know you might get the death penalty  just for the trafficking wouldn't that  encourage you to try to shoot your way  out and maybe kill a police officer or  innocent bystander anywa